#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166634 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "e8353dba-48d7-5f4f-8ba5-97e4e22b6e44" :question/math-academy-id "e-9050" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0$. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\, \\rm{ m/s}$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\sin^2 x \\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ what differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/f187eff194f136838b67ab12b44f8f6882eafb59885a5c3f440d41492def4a42.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\sin^2 x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = -\\sin^2 x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = -\\sin^2 x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166635 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/id #uuid "49e02115-6211-564b-8221-d5835a98a064" :question/math-academy-id "e-9051" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x \\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s}$ and its acceleration is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\, \\rm{m/s}^2.$ If $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x = 1 \\, \\textrm m.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/4fdd70a4ca80965c0adce9c60be36b0712852b662cff69f9733f50c739fd1b32.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x + e^{x-2}) \\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x + e^{x-2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x-2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2 = x^2 + e^{x-2} + C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v = 4 \\, \\rm{m/s}$ when $x = 2 \\, \\textrm m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2}+{e}^{2-2}+C \\\\ 8 & =4+1+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+{e}^{x-2}+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x) \\gt 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv = \\sqrt{2\\left(x^2 + e^{x-2} + 3\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x = 1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({1}^{2}+{e}^{1-2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2(4+{e}^{-1})} \\\\  & ≈2.96\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/id #uuid "80b653d0-5dff-5621-86ee-e1fd336ad740" :question/math-academy-id "e-9455" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\geq 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\textrm m,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/eca36626e6a0cfb30d293e684483c37ddce6f18d4004c3f77258e9663565d8d7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}\\, \\rm{m/s}^2$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =-\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = -\\dfrac{2}{(x+1)^2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=0\\, \\textrm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =\\frac{2}{0+1}+K \\\\ 2 & =2+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x) > 0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x) = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1)^{3/2} & =2t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = \\dfrac{3C_1}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/2} =3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/2} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{2/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{2/3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832f5079-56a5-5633-a36a-abcaa350c6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163437} {:answer/id #uuid "ece83403-1738-5762-a14b-2869fedf272b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} = 6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163438} {:answer/id #uuid "032ae97f-c542-562e-bb45-fad43b5fc7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163439} {:answer/id #uuid "342bdb71-912c-58bd-a61f-2bf43bf15640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dx} =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163440} {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "366f26c4-1e76-5037-be3b-fe4e333ae3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}v^{2}) =-6x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5043cace-708e-584e-9f93-2239e526e2c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde0f0e6-e59d-5767-8e42-f1f093f582b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108010" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $6{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/52fc197612dac35373ecebf771b8444505902b5c6e00c7c55d2b21dab4809136.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $6{x}^{3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-6{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6{x}^{3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-6{x}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "842e9899-302b-5dd9-8481-d1094b937662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317644} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff6f1ec-e0d6-5b2c-ad5d-278cb8da57d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317645} {:answer/id #uuid "ee7a1812-72e5-51db-9fb6-2726504f27db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317646} {:answer/id #uuid "95b73473-19d4-5ad3-81f0-64bca4f1615e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317647} {:answer/id #uuid "e521a1d6-fab9-5059-9480-623a4d36de5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee7a1812-72e5-51db-9fb6-2726504f27db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317646} :answer-field/id #uuid "22a0bea9-2709-505d-b288-e5d8abeb1717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3af95e61-0754-5014-8099-c3f11f615d2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/473a3f91d91351499d5849ee07a86cfcfc2b257949d6ebba68b3261201f2bf68.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceb8256-c17a-53e1-ac22-32f6f795df58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314069} {:answer/id #uuid "1d18169b-8026-562c-a919-850efef7b461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314070} {:answer/id #uuid "b177d108-a207-50df-9c89-8b321579c5ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314071} {:answer/id #uuid "f9552b99-b391-5ece-9344-829c730e0c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314072} {:answer/id #uuid "01416141-3538-5a15-97ee-0eb2e61d5c55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi +t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d18169b-8026-562c-a919-850efef7b461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314070} :answer-field/id #uuid "01399d4a-955d-5d24-b875-b8a046ac00c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1494f900-5c6d-561b-9ba9-7d9115f8d044" :question/math-academy-id "q-122312" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+4{x}^{2})\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t$ where $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}].$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/59c4a51d419b6e42633579b687cde16a2842895c589f818942c10aa304bc98a7.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+4{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2x & =t+K \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=0,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡2t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30dda7a5-4016-5e40-9224-3c6279cdd41c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314076} {:answer/id #uuid "db3b5ecf-1216-57f7-94c9-70c9b7c34f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314077} {:answer/id #uuid "7c730bf0-872c-5722-bdec-13ef6599263d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314078} {:answer/id #uuid "2096a1f6-beb6-516f-98b7-c133fda87158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314079} {:answer/id #uuid "762a12c9-05cb-5020-8b40-db0ca1b94098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2096a1f6-beb6-516f-98b7-c133fda87158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314079} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca2bad5-eebc-557a-a58f-638f93fcb85c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05e75a3d-3e2e-5128-ae1f-6d92d30f38f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122313" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/00da8cdff7814df7368ff00924360db24a93934c5ae1c87ceb5daa6b00df37a7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f9e10f-81f8-5eb5-a68a-d90a070f19b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314083} {:answer/id #uuid "9fdc8b3d-cc9f-5b98-ab40-99bb47c0eb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314084} {:answer/id #uuid "02ac20cd-bd74-5742-8b19-0ec599a24f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314085} {:answer/id #uuid "d1def38b-cfcd-58df-8efc-8af24f5e934a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314086} {:answer/id #uuid "24cf1ab2-5761-5250-bf0d-c8c08c4244a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fdc8b3d-cc9f-5b98-ab40-99bb47c0eb58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314084} :answer-field/id #uuid "4759492e-6d28-5a9e-b0c3-df20068a67e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fca9354-5321-5542-b5b1-39625f1faa01" :question/math-academy-id "q-122315" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/785776cee4f82c5bfa5e86b5b1cb746b7c80f2d702fb52f67055a60bdff249ae.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(3{e}^{x}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{x}+1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=3{e}^{x}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "669596c6-f9a3-5496-bd8e-b3e459f53ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{(t+1)^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317651} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6a5638-dced-58ba-a259-33d911b80871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t-1}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317652} {:answer/id #uuid "7616498e-cebd-5be4-8011-3124669d18a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317653} {:answer/id #uuid "313a6fb7-42fc-56cb-b0ab-d0efca5dd779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c37b3b-f70a-5ea2-b27a-33dfc1bbe80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{1-t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "313a6fb7-42fc-56cb-b0ab-d0efca5dd779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a5db30e-a0e2-5851-8372-5c6ca4a6821c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bb25288-c9b6-561e-ae4a-285d8dc9e994" :question/math-academy-id "q-122316" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/2093298b6cacb0b29f874d401ccca40560517ff5887763e9a2b88bf4bc051abb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}} \\\\ (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (1+x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(1+x)^{3} & =t+K \\\\ (1+x)^{3} & =3t+C \\\\ 1+x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{3t+C}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\sqrt[3]{3t+1}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bda03f20-fb07-520a-aaf5-60370c5b4be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317658} {:answer/id #uuid "b9667457-5964-51f9-9851-0e5e6d259746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317659} {:answer/id #uuid "edd303ab-1073-5b38-a8a8-52b72f58b8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660} {:answer/id #uuid "f354370d-c56e-54be-b3a4-78718e091284" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317661} {:answer/id #uuid "b0eb6f63-59c8-50cd-96c9-e9ac87e7c349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edd303ab-1073-5b38-a8a8-52b72f58b8d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660} :answer-field/id #uuid "90e2f6b7-0b31-5eea-a3f5-f8877dcb0de3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1e05cd3-42be-5ccc-be9a-5e8c9360a4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122319" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/b23ef6e4df05874a715271c578dfa57d5ddacabef2d0801edc4e736c7de66e17.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f114cf7-d9d8-502f-8221-a1c958ebc08d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=1-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317665} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c17a24-052c-57c1-ac80-badfdd88388a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317666} {:answer/id #uuid "2fc95960-d97a-5aa3-8fc3-3829b57ecdd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317667} {:answer/id #uuid "7823de0b-bc02-5710-ba68-7dfc65820aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{2t}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317668} {:answer/id #uuid "46c44727-bec2-5e26-b27d-764191366310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c17a24-052c-57c1-ac80-badfdd88388a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317666} :answer-field/id #uuid "e57444e6-644c-564b-a9d8-eafa62135874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc2a51a2-1b3a-5ed7-9bac-b10855cb9d10" :question/math-academy-id "q-122321" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(4-2x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/7bd0200a651aec7283e5c96d3cdc1589d795531e179ca0eb7cc36ef5bad8de56.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=4-2x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4-2x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4-2x \\\\ \\frac{1}{4-2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{4-2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{4-2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x| & =t+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-x| & =-2t-2K \\\\ 2-x & =C{e}^{-2t} \\\\ x & =2-C{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2-2{e}^{-2t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5acf374f-291a-5639-a46b-7acac2eef5a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314090} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8ec42-40f1-55df-a2d2-6d0cfd24fbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314091} {:answer/id #uuid "01be0c81-03f1-5b16-afe3-01d09fe19fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314092} {:answer/id #uuid "3c860876-8077-5d8a-b490-1124115ea5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314093} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc2e683-94fa-5538-ac1d-59eb33666c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8f8ec42-40f1-55df-a2d2-6d0cfd24fbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314091} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1a84703-3c19-5a54-b80b-5eeb8b58eb55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f818b71-1c62-567d-b5ef-4300712765ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122325" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)={e}^{-2x}\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/8faba5a783797f7025f51ec1ae587cb68355dff7138278689346c2f713275562.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{-2x}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x} & =t+K \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =2t+C \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56fe543e-e026-5266-a271-24932d9b1e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314097} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4a2c70-469e-5ffc-87f6-84c5cbbc4d59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{3{e}^{x-3}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314098} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3928b8-a77e-5319-b69a-404239609725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=3{e}^{x-3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314099} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cf0967-6ed2-591f-b85d-a076aa8110f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={e}^{x-3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314100} {:answer/id #uuid "b253ed67-676c-5100-b57b-d40fec708aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{{e}^{x-3}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56fe543e-e026-5266-a271-24932d9b1e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314097} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea53bf7f-7eb2-545c-a38e-16d9d8e43c05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6b97a4f6-ee90-5fc9-94b5-f52f95caa841" :question/math-academy-id "q-122337" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $3\\,m$ when its velocity is $2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/164c90893a07ae9657e90b6f2b25e53c9c4927b1f4a076429ba0521a98377f24.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x-3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x-3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int {e}^{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={e}^{x-3}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=3\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & ={e}^{3-3}+C \\\\ 2 & =1+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={e}^{x-3}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({e}^{x-3}+1) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({e}^{x-3}+1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6677d18d-461d-5774-bf35-a28cc70e9477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317672} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe2f99b-40f5-5e51-9bc6-887240cd1389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317673} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbeae64-3753-5847-94fc-fb505e5ba435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317674} {:answer/id #uuid "86636676-279b-543b-a4c5-5708abc7bd97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317675} {:answer/id #uuid "dc864baf-94b1-5d3a-9a55-8f3568d4f3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6677d18d-461d-5774-bf35-a28cc70e9477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317672} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a372b2-40cb-51a3-9ac4-e34e924cc3d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "462ad3ee-a76f-525b-b893-9e4b76726600" :question/math-academy-id "q-122338" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccdd6eb6-30fa-580d-92a3-888c20d92865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{64-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317679} {:answer/id #uuid "e2368140-39a2-5d26-a5a7-5c4c57975e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ea363a-f4d3-54d3-bc2a-9ffbd48302c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317681} {:answer/id #uuid "0184e5c6-1bf0-590c-9ece-2e819ba361e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317682} {:answer/id #uuid "9234e108-cc9b-5b79-a281-a9e3e1a74665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2368140-39a2-5d26-a5a7-5c4c57975e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc59459f-1e90-54cd-84d9-f3f11e7a1a71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b60038c6-cb0f-5d07-b76f-fbce9c0d51b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122339" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $0\\,m$ when its velocity is $-2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f25431ad-c934-5706-bfd0-125d8dba8f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\sqrt{2(3x+5{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317686} {:answer/id #uuid "bb44f4d9-5072-591e-ba32-ab92138a6bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-(2+x+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317687} {:answer/id #uuid "b96918df-5265-54d6-9385-017cf6ff4704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{3x+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317688} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0d9fd1-76cc-5606-9381-3d41b0ed4fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317689} {:answer/id #uuid "08c6510b-d1c2-5f66-821c-559b0458025e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2(x+{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e0d9fd1-76cc-5606-9381-3d41b0ed4fc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317689} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dc1eca9-9de0-57d7-bc63-4a4743a75b6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab95451c-4d9a-5333-846f-3dc7dd4ef9b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-122340" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $-4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/466c0aa389beb6d1a42092b73bae75508ef2c50f1f08cca5efaac20d25615f30.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4(1+2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4(1+2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int 4(1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4(x+{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-4)^{2} & =4(1+{1}^{2})+C \\\\ 8 & =8+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4(x+{x}^{2})+0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8(x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-2\\sqrt{2(x+{x}^{2})}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e007c7-6cc3-5e98-99e9-dc4b2cdd07ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317693} {:answer/id #uuid "64345185-5abe-5170-9e41-b6e3f42d7d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317694} {:answer/id #uuid "fe150ef1-3540-5259-be8b-b205591045c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317695} {:answer/id #uuid "3db94eb2-4153-5552-b898-d5c7e4d753b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317696} {:answer/id #uuid "9dae4abf-bd8d-5985-8531-89977efe3c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64345185-5abe-5170-9e41-b6e3f42d7d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317694} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2f47d5-7302-5cb4-88e6-fef841d5bf39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7832c005-e5d4-5bbe-91b5-55956ce35ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122342" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8540e57d-b048-5ab3-aeaa-e7c48698f013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314132} {:answer/id #uuid "5f73179e-8a65-5462-9098-cbd299e605f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314133} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9d27b1-016c-540e-9806-3c428ea3a9f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314134} {:answer/id #uuid "c168ec6d-57b5-5f35-abb9-4c56118eb100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314135} {:answer/id #uuid "f391d876-9bc8-51c8-8822-3545affef68b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f73179e-8a65-5462-9098-cbd299e605f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314133} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6e123a-1fab-58ba-a491-c61f45ab4aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b41d621c-b89a-58ab-8c66-2280c7bf01d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-122349" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
